WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.417
ALEXIS MOUSSINE-POUCHKINE:
Can you

00:00:01.417 --> 00:00:03.970
get a sense of Google Cloud
Platform data processing

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:06.525
and data storage products
in just a few minutes?

00:00:06.525 --> 00:00:07.150
Let's find out.

00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:10.974
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:18.230
It all starts with the ability
to store objects or files.

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:21.440
Google Cloud Storage is a
fully-managed object storage

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:22.460
service.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:25.820
It offers different storage
classes for multi-regional,

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:28.460
for optimal and user
latency, to coldline storage

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:30.680
for lower frequency access.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:34.040
There is a single unified API
to interact with cloud storage.

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:35.840
This makes, for
instance, moving objects

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:39.140
across storage classes
completely seamless.

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:41.900
Cloud storage offers
strong consistency,

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:44.390
and is designed for 11
lines of durability.

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:47.930
Storing data is great,
but processing it

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:50.590
is probably even more useful.

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:54.760
In the big data category,
a GCP "hello world"

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:58.720
would be to throw an SQL query
at a large set of data, hoping

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:01.070
to find useful insights.

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:04.430
Let's do just that
with BigQuery.

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:08.030
You could, of course, upload
and work with your own data set,

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:12.480
but here we will use sample data
from a public data set instead.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:14.850
What's important here
is that at no point

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:19.010
do you need to provision a
cluster or storage capacity.

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:21.770
Simply craft a
standard SQL query,

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:23.600
and within seconds,
BigQuery will

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.780
have processed potentially
gigantic amounts of data

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:30.260
and produced a result that can
be saved in various formats.

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:32.750
For instance, using the
public data set of GitHub

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:35.120
commits, here we've
asked BigQuery

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:37.550
to provide the list of
the top Google employees

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:39.620
fixing stuff on GitHub.

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:42.530
Also with the same data
set, we can figure out

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:45.720
which programming languages
make developers the most happy.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:48.440
Notice the regular expression
embedded in the query.

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:51.170
The key point here is how
much data is processed

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:53.330
in such a short period of time.

00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:57.290
You can import or stream data
to BigQuery using an API,

00:01:57.290 --> 00:01:59.660
but also export just
about any data produced

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:01.860
by other GCP products
and services,

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:04.110
such as logs to BigQuery.

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:08.479
If you have existing Apache
Hadoop or Spark workloads,

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:10.460
you will likely be
interested in trying out

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:13.940
Cloud Dataproc, GCP's
fully-managed environment that

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:17.660
will spin up a cluster
in less than 90 seconds.

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:22.400
Let's talk about relational
databases, or RDBMSes SQL

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.800
as a language still has
unsurpassed qualities,

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:30.420
and RDBMSes, such as MySQL and
PostgreSQL are very popular.

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:32.750
This is why Google
Cloud SQL is a managed

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:36.090
service that supports both of
these relational databases.

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:37.940
This is a great
solution for storing

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:40.920
your existing or new
application data.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:44.010
Simply choose between
MySQL and PostgreSQL.

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:47.610
Select a machine size with up
to hundreds of gigabytes of RAM

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:48.930
and terabytes of storage.

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:51.720
Select a zone, and
cloud SQL will take care

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:53.310
of providing the following--

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:55.515
encryption at rest
and in transit,

00:02:55.515 --> 00:02:58.770
a private IP address,
data replication

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:01.860
between multiple zones
with automatic failover,

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:07.160
automated backups, as well
as point-in-time recovery.

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:09.620
Staying with
relational databases,

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:12.140
Google also offers
Cloud Spanner,

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:14.840
a horizontally scalable,
strongly consistent

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:17.180
relational database
as a service.

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:19.910
Make sure to check out this
truly innovative technology

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:23.150
that defies the CAP theorem and
that many Google services are

00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:24.790
built on.

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:27.370
In the NoSQL department,
Cloud Firestore

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:29.860
is a highly scalable,
strongly consistent database

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:31.400
offering two modes--

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:33.640
the native mode offering
real time updates

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:36.010
and offline support
for mobile developers,

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:38.170
and the datastore mode
for backend developers

00:03:38.170 --> 00:03:41.950
looking for a schema-less
documents database.

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:44.500
Cloud Bigtable will complement
your storage options

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:46.580
with the petabyte
scale fully managed,

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:48.610
no escrow database service.

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:51.160
This will be mostly suited
for large analytical and

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:53.680
operational workloads as it
comes with high throughput

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:57.130
characteristics such as
consistent sub-10 millisecond

00:03:57.130 --> 00:03:58.360
latency.

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:02.980
In any case, you should visit
cloudatgoogle.com/storageoptions

00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:05.290
for a decision tree to
understand which is the best

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:06.570
database for you.

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:10.150
GCP has numerous other
data-related products,

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:12.340
some particularly innovative.

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:14.570
Be sure to consider
the following.

00:04:14.570 --> 00:04:17.110
Pub/Sub for producing
and consuming messages

00:04:17.110 --> 00:04:20.250
globally across all
GCP zones and regions.

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:24.250
Filestore for applications that
require a system interface.

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:26.260
Cloud Memorystore
for Redis provides

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:29.590
a fully managed, in-memory
data storage service

00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:31.300
that is ideal to
build application

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:35.470
caches with sub-millisecond
data access.

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:38.080
Cloud Dataflow to
build and execute

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:40.630
unified batch and
streaming pipelines

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:43.770
using the Apache Beam
programming model.

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:46.800
Dataprep to make sure
your data scientists

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:50.070
are not spending 90% of
their time cleaning up data

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:52.260
instead of processing it.

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:56.100
And Data Studio, for interactive
dashboards and engaging

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:57.660
reports.

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:00.840
Consider taking free codelabs
linked in the description below

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:04.050
to explore any of
these GCP products.

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:07.830
Check out the compute overview
and machine learning episodes,

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:11.670
and look forward to an upcoming
video on DevOps and tooling.

00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:14.940
If you like this video, please
like, subscribe, comment,

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:15.960
and share.

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:18.120
See you soon for another
GCP Essentials video.

